# #12 (owner-confirmed 2026-08-19 06:09): the JIT stack-calibration question must
# use PLAIN wording, never the internal "base(시작 무게) / step(한 칸 간격)" terms
# the user flagged as too hard (the wording was too technical). When a
# machine/stack slot is not
# yet calibrated (weight_kg null -> the stack has never been measured), the entry
# surfaces the plain two-option ask UP FRONT so the model never hand-rolls a
# base/step question mid-set. This is the deterministic, code-provided surface
# form -- the model echoes it verbatim (like CONFIRMED_SETTINGS). It does NOT
# register the user's equipment inventory (that is #40 resource-inventory, a
# separate schema design -- FLAGGED, not built here); it only fixes the WORDING
# of the unavoidable JIT ask and surfaces it at entry rather than mid-set.
_JIT_STACK_ASK = ("이 기구 스택 눈금을 알려주시거나(예: 5, 10, 15 ...), "
                  "기구 사진 한 장 찍어 보내주세요")


def needs_stack_calibration(st, db_path, exercise_name, slot):
    """#12: True iff this slot is a machine/stack lift that is not yet
    calibrated (no working weight on record). Such a slot forces the on-demand
    JIT stack-learning ask; surfacing it AT ENTRY with plain wording (see
    _JIT_STACK_ASK) is the wording fix. Bodyweight / freeweight / already-weighted
    slots return False (no calibration ask). Best-effort: any error -> False."""
    if not isinstance(slot, dict):
        return False
    # A1: a hold is never a stack calibration.
    if _is_hold_slot(slot, db_path):
        return False
    # A4: the stack is known once a working load was LOGGED for this lift (the
    # progression base, program state step A) or the card declares a seed.
    # The seed alone used to decide, so a lift calibrated in session 1 was
    # asked again in session 2.
    if slot.get("weight_kg") is not None:
        return False
    try:
        base, _src = _rx_for_hold().resolve_base_weight(
            db_path, exercise_name, None)
        if base is not None:
            return False
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
        pass
    try:
        _movement, load_type = st._exercise_class(db_path, exercise_name)
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
        return False
    # only machine/stack loads have a numeric stack to calibrate; a bodyweight /
    # freeweight null-weight slot is a calibration of REPS or a bar load, not a
    # stack number -> no stack-eumgeum ask.
    return load_type in ("machine", "stack")


def extract_record_params(slot):
    """#28 + #24/#25: record-backed params that must be auto-applied, never
    re-asked. Currently: unilateral performance mode + weak-side execution.

    SOURCE PRECEDENCE (the #24/#25 fix -- the inconsistency root was that this
    used ONLY a prose keyword scan, so some unilateral slots hit "약한쪽"/"싱글레그"
    and others did not -> the agent asked weak-side for some and not others):
      1. The EXPLICIT program.yaml `unilateral` field is authoritative when
         present -- a structured {mode, weak_side_first, weak_side} block. This
         is deterministic: every unilateral-capable slot carries its performance
         mode, so the behavior is identical across all of them (no guessing).
      2. The prose keyword scan is the FALLBACK only, for a JIT / unregistered
         slot that has no explicit field yet (graceful, backward-compatible).

    Emitted params:
      unilateral      = the performance mode (both_one_record | ...). Its presence
                        tells the model this is a unilateral slot (auto-loaded).
      weak_side_first = yes when 약한 쪽 먼저 protocol applies (강한 쪽은 약한 쪽
                        반복수에 맞춤). Absent when the mode has no weak-first order
                        (e.g. bulgarian split squat -- a balance split, not an
                        imbalance-correction slot).
      weak_side       = the concrete weaker side (left|right) WHEN recorded. Never
                        guessed here; a null recorded side emits confirm_side=yes
                        so the model confirms it ONCE, then it is auto-loaded and
                        never re-asked.
      confirm_side    = yes iff weak_side_first applies but the concrete side is
                        not yet recorded (weak_side null) -- the ONE-TIME confirm
                        gate. Absent once weak_side is recorded (never re-ask).
    Returns a dict; never raises.

    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    2026-09-11, INCIDENT B -- THE EXECUTION MODE IS NOW ALWAYS ON THE WIRE.
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    standing_calf_raise declares no `unilateral` block, so this function used to
    return {} for it and the caller printed `RECORD_PARAMS none`. Two minutes
    after announcing a 115kg load, the turn announced a switch to single-leg
    execution on that same lift. 「아니그거 시티드였는데.. 스탠딩은 양발이야」.
    At 115kg 양발 -> 한발 doubles the load per leg.

    Nothing in the data suggested it. `RECORD_PARAMS none` is not data -- it is
    a vacuum, and the ruling is that a vacuum on this axis is now impossible:

        exec_mode        ALWAYS present. The DECLARED mode when program.yaml
                         carries one; `bilateral` when it does not. There is no
                         third outcome and no absent state.
        exec_mode_source declared | default -- so the owner can tell a READ from
                         a DEFAULT and overrule either.

    The answer itself is not decided here: set-table.resolve_unilateral is the
    one resolver (built on unilateral_spec, the one parser), and this function
    only carries its answer onto the wire. A second reader is how the CUE path
    and the INPUT path said two different things about the same slot on
    2026-09-02.

    THE PROSE SCAN IS DEMOTED, NOT DELETED. It used to be able to SET
    `unilateral` from keywords in a JIT slot's safety_note. Under this ruling
    an inference cannot be the answer -- 「선언이 없으면 답은 양측이다」 -- so a
    keyword hit now emits `exec_mode_hint` instead: a machine-only signal that
    an unregistered slot may need a declaration, sitting BESIDE an exec_mode
    that still reads `bilateral`. The information survives; its authority does
    not. Declaring the slot in program.yaml is what promotes a hint to an
    answer, and that is a data edit the owner makes, not one a turn makes.

    The weak-side half of the scan is UNCHANGED: 약한 쪽 먼저 is an execution
    ORDER, a different axis from how many limbs move, and #28's auto-load of it
    is not what broke today."""
    out = {}
    if not slot:
        return out

    # (0) THE EXECUTION MODE -- always, from the one resolver.
    # Best-effort: if set-table cannot be loaded the entry must still happen, and
    # a MISSING exec_mode line degrades to exactly the pre-2026-09-11 behaviour
    # rather than to a wrong one.
    try:
        st = _load_module("set_table", "set-table")
        exec_spec = st.resolve_unilateral(slot)
        out["exec_mode"] = exec_spec["mode"]
        out["exec_mode_source"] = exec_spec["source"]
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 -- never block an entry on this
        exec_spec = None

    uni = slot.get("unilateral")
    if isinstance(uni, dict):
        # (1) EXPLICIT field -- deterministic, authoritative.
        mode = uni.get("mode")
        out["unilateral"] = str(mode) if mode else "yes"
        if uni.get("weak_side_first"):
            out["weak_side_first"] = "yes"
            side = uni.get("weak_side")
            if side:
                # Concrete weaker side is on record -> auto-load, NEVER re-ask.
                out["weak_side"] = str(side)
            else:
                # Weaker-side protocol applies but the concrete side is not yet
                # recorded -> confirm it ONCE, then it becomes a recorded param.
                out["confirm_side"] = "yes"
        return out

    # (2) FALLBACK prose keyword scan (JIT / unregistered slot without the field).
    # A4: the old `slot.last.result` half is gone -- no writer ever filled it
    # (program-init never renders it), so the scan reads the safety note only.
    hay = str(slot.get("safety_note") or "")

    if _has_term(hay, WEAK_SIDE_TERMS):
        # The recorded protocol for these slots is: weak side first, strong side
        # matches the weak side's rep count (owner-confirmed 2026-07-17,
        # encoded in leg_curl / single_leg_extension safety_note + prefs).
        out["weak_side_first"] = "yes"
        out["confirm_side"] = "yes"
    if _has_term(hay, UNILATERAL_TERMS):
        # A HINT, never the answer (see the docstring). exec_mode stays
        # `bilateral:default` beside it.
        out["exec_mode_hint"] = "undeclared_unilateral_prose"
    return out


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# (f) #10/#11/#26 -- PREP-PHASE BUCKETS (F2). Prep drills before a working set
# were being FRONT-LOADED under live pressure: on v_squat entry the model
# presented K1 (equipment prep) AND K4 (내전근 아이소메트릭 immediate-primer)
# up-front and even armed K4's timer -- but K4 must fire IMMEDIATELY BEFORE the
# first working set, not at entry ("워킹셋 직전이라면서 왜 지금하는데"). FIX: each
# slot's prep drills carry a deterministic PHASE bucket; exercise-enter reads
# them, orders by phase, and emits two SEPARATE sections so the model presents
# entry-time drills at entry but DEFERS immediate-primers to just before the
# first working set. This is the ORDER SoT (the drill prose glossary stays in
# stretch-matrix.md); the model no longer decides the phase.
#
# 2026-08-21 collapse: the DRILL DATA no longer lives in program.yaml. The one
# canonical prep source is files/program/prep.yaml; do_enter() hydrates this
# slot's `prep_drills` from it via rx-calc's prep_slot_drills(day, slot) before
# calling extract_prep_plan. The functions below are unchanged and still take a
# slot dict carrying `prep_drills` -- only WHERE that list comes from moved, so
# the emitted PREP_* lines are byte-identical.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The 3 F2 prep phases, in the deterministic order they are performed. A drill
# whose phase is absent/unknown falls to the END of its own emitted section so a
# data typo never silently drops it (fail-visible, not fail-open).
#   mat_prep         = general session-start warmup (owned by session open, not
#                      an exercise entry -- surfaced here only if a slot declares
#                      it, for completeness).
#   equipment_prep   = slot-immediate prep needing equipment (e.g. K1 무릎
#                      스크루홈, bench setup), performed at ENTRY before warmup.
#   immediate_primer = performed RIGHT BEFORE the first working set (e.g. K4
#                      내전근 아이소), AFTER cues + set-table + warmup/ramp.
PREP_PHASE_ORDER = ["mat_prep", "equipment_prep", "immediate_primer"]


def _phase_rank(phase):
    """Deterministic sort key: a known phase sorts by PREP_PHASE_ORDER, an
    unknown/None phase sorts LAST (large index) so it is never lost, just tail."""
    try:
        return PREP_PHASE_ORDER.index(phase)
    except ValueError:
        return len(PREP_PHASE_ORDER)


def extract_prep_plan(slot):
    """#10/#11/#26 (F2): read the slot's prep_drills, bucket by phase, and return
    an ORDERED plan split into the two emission sections:
        {"entry": [...], "immediate_primer": [...]}
    'entry' = drills the model presents at exercise entry (mat_prep +
    equipment_prep + any unknown/typo phase, kept fail-visible), in phase order
    then declared order. 'immediate_primer' = ONLY known immediate_primer-phase
    drills, DEFERRED to just before the first working set. Each item is a dict
    {code, phase, display, hold_sec?} carried verbatim from prep.yaml, the
    canonical prep SoT (no invention -- do_enter hydrates the slot from it).
    Never raises; a slot with no prep_drills -> both lists empty."""
    plan = {"entry": [], "immediate_primer": []}
    if not slot:
        return plan
    drills = slot.get("prep_drills")
    if not isinstance(drills, list):
        return plan
    # Sort by phase rank only. Python's sort is STABLE, so drills within the same
    # phase keep their program.yaml declared order (no explicit index tie-break
    # needed), while phases order deterministically by PREP_PHASE_ORDER.
    ordered = sorted(
        [d for d in drills if isinstance(d, dict)],
        key=lambda d: _phase_rank(d.get("phase")),
    )
    for d in ordered:
        item = {
            "code": d.get("code"),
            "phase": d.get("phase"),
            "display": d.get("display"),
        }
        if d.get("hold_sec") is not None:
            item["hold_sec"] = d.get("hold_sec")
        # A3/D1: the prescription travels with the drill. Absent stays ABSENT
        # (prep.yaml's contract) -- the table renders `?` and the owner closes
        # it in one word; nothing is defaulted in.
        if d.get("target_reps") is not None:
            item["target_reps"] = d.get("target_reps")
        # 2026-08-27: the UNIT travels with the value. `target_reps` is one
        # integer column carrying four incompatible units (total reps / per-side
        # reps / rounds / hold sets); dropping the unit here is what let a round
        # count reach the DB as a rep count. set-table.prep_target_spec is the
        # reader; this whitelist is the seam that must not eat the field.
        if d.get("target_unit") is not None:
            item["target_unit"] = d.get("target_unit")
        if d.get("load_kg") is not None:
            item["load_kg"] = d.get("load_kg")
        if d.get("implement") is not None:
            item["implement"] = d.get("implement")
        # ZONE + REQUIRES (2026-09-11). Owner, live: 「랫풀다운은 매트프렙이아닌데
        # 구분이 전혀안돼있네」 -- the screen showed 준비운동 1 / 2 / 3 and drill 3
        # is at a lat-pulldown machine, a walk away from the mat he was on.
        #
        # The axis was NOT missing from the data. prep.yaml has carried
        # `zone: equipment` + `requires: [lat_pulldown_machine]` on that drill
        # since 2026-09-02, and its header declares the three peer collections
        # and their display names. THIS WHITELIST ATE IT. The comment three
        # lines up already names the failure mode -- "this whitelist is the seam
        # that must not eat the field" -- written when `target_unit` was eaten
        # the same way. Same seam, next field.
        #
        # No new axis is introduced here. The declared one is carried through.
        for _k in ("zone", "requires"):
            if d.get(_k) is not None:
                item[_k] = d.get(_k)
        # THE CUE (W3, owner 2026-08-25 and again 2026-09-11): a prep drill is a
        # set, and a set gets a cue. prep.yaml has carried `cue.setup` /
        # `cue.sensation` per drill since 2026-09-02 -- setup is what the body
        # does, sensation is what it should feel like -- and NOTHING emitted
        # them. At the moment the owner is told to do the drill, the one field
        # that says how to do it was not in the reply. That is the same
        # measured shape as the equipment name disappearing off a drill that
        # used to name it: the data has the answer and the emitter does not
        # send it. Carried as a dict; _prep_cue_lines renders it.
        if isinstance(d.get("cue"), dict) and d.get("cue"):
            item["cue"] = dict(d.get("cue"))
        # Only a KNOWN immediate_primer is DEFERRED to just before the first
        # working set. Everything else -- mat_prep / equipment_prep AND any
        # unknown/typo phase -- goes to the ENTRY section so an unrecognized
        # drill stays VISIBLE at entry (fail-visible), never silently deferred
        # to a phase that might fire it at the wrong moment (the #26 failure was
        # exactly a mis-timed primer).
        if d.get("phase") == "immediate_primer":
            plan["immediate_primer"].append(item)
        else:
            plan["entry"].append(item)
    return plan


